# November 21, 2026: a first scatter across Wyoming (region wy), the state with the fewest places on the map, claimed with
# tools/choose.py --thinnest. The one earlier batch (data_wy_iwfl) worked Carbon County; this one leaves it alone and puts
# one or two places in each corner of the state: the Oregon Trail crossing of the North Platte (Fort Laramie, Register Cliff,
# the Guernsey ruts), the South Pass gold district (South Pass City, Miner's Delight), the southwest (Fort Bridger, the first
# J.C. Penney store at Kemmerer), Jackson Hole (Mormon Row), the Big Horn Basin and Yellowstone (the Irma, Heart Mountain,
# the Old Faithful Inn), Sheridan and the Bozeman Trail (Trail End, the Sheridan Inn, Fort Phil Kearny), the Laramie Plains
# (the territorial prison, the Ames pyramid), Casper and Douglas (Fort Caspar, Fort Fetterman) and Cheyenne's depot.
# Sources were opened in this session: Wikipedia as raw text (tools/wiki_raw.py); through tools/page.py the managers' own
# pages (Wyoming State Parks, the National Park Service, the BLM, the City of Casper's Fort Caspar Museum, the Heart Mountain
# Wyoming Foundation, the Cheyenne Depot Museum, Yellowstone National Park Lodges, the Irma Hotel and the Sheridan Inn) and
# WyoHistory.org.
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
def L(t,u): return [t,u]
BATCH={'key':'wy_udiz','label':'Wyoming: a first scatter','added':'2026-11-21 13:22'}
WSP='https://wyoparks.wyo.gov/index.php/'
PLACES=[
N('fort-laramie-nhs','Fort Laramie','Fort Laramie','wy','fort','standing',42.20362,-104.55746,'site','open','walk','forts','1834–1890',
  'William Sublette and Robert Campbell built a log trading post here in 1834 where the Laramie River meets the North Platte; the American Fur Company rebuilt it in adobe as Fort John in 1841. The Army bought it in June 1849 and it became the great stop on the Oregon Trail, and the place where the Fort Laramie treaties of 1851 and 1868 were made. The post closed in 1890, and the government bought the site back in 1938 to preserve it.',
  'Old Bedlam, the officers\' quarters of 1849, is the oldest known U.S. military building in Wyoming. Also standing: the post trader\'s store, the old guardhouse of 1866 and a second guardhouse, the Burt House restored to 1887-1888, and the ruins of the 1871 hospital and many other buildings (what stands is about a third of what was there). Several buildings are furnished; which are open on a given day was not checked.',
  'The fur post that became the Army\'s busiest stop on the Oregon Trail.',
  'Fort Laramie National Historic Site, National Park Service, about 3 miles southwest of the town of Fort Laramie. Free: no entrance pass needed. Grounds open sunrise to sunset every day; the Fort Museum and Visitor Center is open daily except Thanksgiving, Christmas and New Year\'s Day (NPS hours page, read November 2026). Parking in the visitor center lot off Gray Rocks Road.',
  [W('Fort Laramie National Historic Site'),L('NPS: Fort Laramie operating hours','https://www.nps.gov/fola/planyourvisit/hours.htm'),L('NPS: Fort Laramie fees','https://www.nps.gov/fola/planyourvisit/fees.htm')],
  th=[42.20418,-104.55876],thn='Visitor center lot',brick='?',hours='Grounds sunrise to sunset daily. Visitor center daily 8 a.m. to 6 p.m. Memorial Day to Labor Day, 5 p.m. in May and September, 4 p.m. October to April'),
N('register-cliff-guernsey','Register Cliff','Guernsey','wy','landmark','standing',42.2476,-104.71136,'exact','public','walk','','1840s–1869',
  'A soft rock cliff over the North Platte where emigrants on the Oregon, California and Mormon trails camped and carved their names, one of three such "registers" in Wyoming with Independence Rock and Names Hill. Wikipedia calls it sandstone in one place and limestone in another.',
  'Names and dates cut into the cliff face by emigrants; how the path runs and which inscriptions are protected was not checked.',
  'Read the names the emigrants left on the rock.',
  'Register Cliff State Historic Site, managed by Guernsey State Park, 2 miles south of Guernsey on the south bank of the North Platte. Open all year, sunrise to sunset (Wyoming State Parks, read November 2026). Phone 307-836-2334. Small lot at the cliff on South Guernsey Road.',
  [W('Register Cliff'),L('Wyoming State Parks: About Register Cliff',WSP+'about-register-cliff')],
  th=[42.24762,-104.71174],thn='Register Cliff lot, South Guernsey Road',brick='no',hours='All year, sunrise to sunset'),
N('oregon-trail-ruts-guernsey','Oregon Trail Ruts','Guernsey','wy','landmark','traces',42.25611,-104.74944,'site','public','walk','','1841–1869',
  'Wagons, animals and people wore the Oregon Trail two to six feet down into a sandstone ridge here between 1841 and 1869. The half-mile stretch is called the best-preserved set of trail ruts anywhere on its length. It was made a National Historic Landmark in 1966.',
  'A half mile of ruts cut deep into the rock of the ridge, reached by a short walk from the lot.',
  'Wheel ruts cut shoulder-deep into rock.',
  'Oregon Trail Ruts State Historic Site, about a mile south of Guernsey (I-25 exit 92 to US 26); Guernsey State Park manages it. Open all year, sunrise to sunset (Wyoming State Parks, read November 2026). Phone 307-836-2334.',
  [W('Oregon Trail Ruts'),L('Wyoming State Parks: About Oregon Trail Ruts',WSP+'about-oregon-trail-ruts')],
  th=[42.25713,-104.74919],thn='Oregon Trail Ruts lot, River Drive',brick='no',hours='All year, sunrise to sunset'),
N('south-pass-city-wy','South Pass City','South Pass City','wy','town','standing',42.46833,-108.79972,'site','open','walk','','1867–',
  'Gold was found nearby in 1867 and prospecting on what became the Carissa mine began a year later; within a year some 2,000 people lived here. William Bright, a saloon keeper elected from South Pass City, brought in the bill that made Wyoming in 1869 the first place in the country to give women the vote, and Esther Hobart Morris of the town became the first woman justice of the peace in 1870. The boom faded and the town emptied.',
  'The state historic site keeps more than 30 buildings from the 1860s and 1870s. The state bought the Carissa Mine in 2003; its mine buildings can be toured with demonstrations of mining and milling machinery. A handful of people still live in the rest of the town.',
  'The gold camp that gave American women their first vote.',
  'South Pass City State Historic Site, 125 South Pass Main Street, 2 miles south of the junction of Wyoming 28 and 131. Wyoming State Parks (read November 2026): historic buildings and gift shop open daily 10 a.m. to 5 p.m. from Memorial Day to mid-October; the site and its trails are open sunrise to sunset all year, weather permitting. Phone 307-332-3684; southpasscity.org has current events. Fees not checked.',
  [W('South Pass City, Wyoming'),L('Wyoming State Parks: About South Pass City',WSP+'about-south-pass-city')],
  brick='?',hours='Buildings: Memorial Day to mid-October, daily 10 a.m. to 5 p.m. Site and trails: sunrise to sunset all year, weather permitting'),
N('miners-delight-wy','Miner\'s Delight (Hamilton City)','Atlantic City','wy','town','ruins',42.53306,-108.68056,'site','public','walk','','1868–1960',
  'Gold was found here in 1868, and Hamilton City, known as Miner\'s Delight after its mine, had 75 people by 1870, forty of them miners. It went through several booms and busts, the mine shutting in 1882 and reopening after 1900, with small revivals in 1907, 1910 and the 1930s. People lived here until about 1960.',
  'Seventeen log and rough-lumber structures stand: seven cabins, a saloon, a meat house, a shop or barn, a shaft house, a pantry, a cellar, three privies and a corral (Wikipedia, from the BLM). A rugged walking path runs through the site with an interpretive kiosk at the entrance and panels inside; the BLM says it is not wheelchair accessible.',
  'A log ghost town the BLM keeps standing.',
  'Bureau of Land Management, Lander Field Office; no fee. The BLM\'s directions: County Road 237 north from Atlantic City, then east on BLM Road 2324 (Fort Stambaugh Loop Road) for 2 miles, then follow the signs north. Gravel and two-track roads at high altitude; winter access not checked.',
  [W('Miner\'s Delight, Wyoming'),L('BLM: Miner\'s Delight','https://www.blm.gov/visit/miners-delight')],
  conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('fort-bridger-wy','Fort Bridger','Fort Bridger','wy','fort','standing',41.3181,-110.39127,'site','open','walk','forts','1843–1890',
  'Jim Bridger and Louis Vasquez set up a trading post on Blacks Fork of the Green River in 1842-1843, a resupply stop on the Oregon, California and Mormon trails. Mormons held it in the 1850s and built a cobblestone wall; when the Army came in 1857 during the Utah War it found the post burned inside that wall. The Army kept a post here from 1858 until 1890, and the state took the site in 1928.',
  'Standing: the Mormon wall, the old Pony Express barn, and later Army buildings (the 1888 barracks served as a dairy milk barn and now holds the state museum). Wyoming State Parks interprets five eras here, from the mountain men to the motel and museum.',
  'Jim Bridger\'s trading post, the Mormons\' wall and the Army\'s fort in one place.',
  'Fort Bridger State Historic Site, 37000 I-80 Business Loop, about three miles off I-80 at exit 34. Wyoming State Parks (read November 2026): museum and historic buildings daily 9 a.m. to 5 p.m. from May 1 to September 30, closed to the public October to April except by appointment; grounds open all year, sunrise to sunset. Office 307-782-3842. Fees not checked.',
  [W('Fort Bridger'),L('Wyoming State Parks: About Fort Bridger',WSP+'about-fort-bridger')],
  th=[41.318,-110.38972],thn='Fort Bridger lot, Business Loop 80',brick='?',hours='Museum and buildings May 1 to September 30, daily 9 a.m. to 5 p.m. (October to April by appointment). Grounds sunrise to sunset all year'),
N('jc-penney-district-kemmerer','J.C. Penney mother store and house','Kemmerer','wy','civic','standing',41.79499,-110.53675,'exact','open','walk','','c. 1900–',
  'James Cash Penney opened his Golden Rule Store in Kemmerer in 1902 on Pine Street (that building is gone) and moved in 1904 into a stone building on South Main Street with a marble and cast-iron front, which became the "mother store" of the J.C. Penney chain. His family lived from 1902 to 1909 in the small frame house across the street, which he owned until his death in 1971. The district was made a National Historic Landmark in 1978.',
  'The two-story stone store building on South Main Street, next to a false-front building, and across J.C. Penney Drive the 1-1/2-story clapboard house, now a house museum in a small park. Whether the old store still trades was not checked: Wikipedia says the company\'s Kemmerer store is a short distance away.',
  'Where the J.C. Penney chain began.',
  'J.C. Penney House museum, 722 J.C. Penney Drive (WyoHistory.org\'s address; OpenStreetMap gives 105). WyoHistory.org\'s field-trip page, undated, gives the museum as open Monday to Saturday 9 a.m. to 6 p.m. and closed Sunday, phone 307-877-3164; that is not the operator\'s own page, so check before going. Street parking; a lot on Lignite Street.',
  [W('J. C. Penney Historic District'),L('WyoHistory.org: J.C. Penney House field trip','https://wyohistory.org/field-trips/jc-penney-house')],
  th=[41.79554,-110.53763],thn='Lot on Lignite Street',conf='mixed',brick='?',hours='?'),
N('mormon-row-grand-teton','Mormon Row and the Moulton barns','Moose','wy','town','standing',43.66069,-110.665,'exact','public','walk','','1896–1950s',
  'Mormon homesteaders from Idaho settled east of Blacktail Butte in the 1890s, clustering 27 homesteads in a community the post office called Grovont, and dug irrigation ditches by hand and by team between 1896 and 1937. Most families later sold to the park. The row was listed as a historic district in 1997.',
  'T.A. Moulton\'s gable-with-shed barn, the one photographers line up against the Tetons, and north of Antelope Flats Road John Moulton\'s two-story gambrel barn and pink stucco house. The Andy Chambers homestead, claimed in 1912, is the most complete group and its windmill still stands; water still runs in some of the ditches. Barns seen from outside; interiors not checked.',
  'The barn in front of the Tetons, and the homesteads around it.',
  'Grand Teton National Park. From Jackson drive north on US 191 past Moose Junction, turn right on Antelope Flats Road and go about 1.5 miles to the dirt road by the pink stucco house, where there is a small dirt lot with a trail brochure and sign (NPS page, read November 2026). The park charges an entrance fee ($20 to $35 a vehicle); whether a gate stands on this road was not checked. Winter road conditions not checked.',
  [W('Mormon Row Historic District'),L('NPS: Mormon Row','https://www.nps.gov/grte/learn/historyculture/mormon.htm')],
  th=[43.66556,-110.6643],thn='Mormon Row lot by the pink house',brick='no'),
N('irma-hotel-cody','Irma Hotel','Cody','wy','hotel','standing',44.52571,-109.06452,'exact','open','drive','hotels','1902–',
  'Buffalo Bill Cody built the hotel in the town he co-founded and named it for his youngest daughter Irma; it opened to the public on November 1, 1902, with a grand party on November 18, and cost $80,000. He signed it over to his wife Louisa in 1913; later owners added an annex for motorists about 1930. It still runs as a hotel and restaurant.',
  'The cherrywood back bar in the restaurant, which the hotel says Queen Victoria gave Cody after a command performance; Cody\'s own two suites, restored and let to guests. The restaurant is open to anyone; its hours were not on the page read.',
  'Buffalo Bill\'s own hotel, with Queen Victoria\'s bar.',
  'A working hotel and restaurant at 1192 Sheridan Avenue, downtown Cody. Phone 307-587-4221. Street parking; whether the lots behind the block are public was not checked.',
  [W('Irma Hotel'),L('The Irma Hotel','https://www.irmahotel.com/')],
  brick='?',hours='?'),
N('heart-mountain-wy','Heart Mountain Relocation Center','Powell','wy','inst','ruins',44.67035,-108.94145,'site','open','drive','','1942–1945',
  'Between Cody and Powell the government built 650 barracks behind guard towers in 1942 to hold Japanese Americans forced from the West Coast. Nearly 14,000 people passed through, with a peak of 10,767, which made it the third-largest town in Wyoming until it closed on November 10, 1945. Part of the site became a National Historic Landmark in 2007, and the Heart Mountain Wyoming Foundation opened an interpretive center in 2011.',
  'Four of the original barracks survive in place. The foundation\'s page points to the camp hospital\'s red brick chimney on the hill as the landmark for the turn. The interpretive center holds photographs, artifacts and oral histories; a walking tour starts west of it.',
  'The camp where 10,000 Japanese Americans were held under Heart Mountain.',
  'Heart Mountain Interpretive Center, 1539 Road 19, Powell, off US 14A about 15 minutes from Cody or Powell; paved lot at the center. Admission (foundation page, read November 2026) adults $14, seniors and students $12, ages 10-17 $10, under 10 free. Phone (307) 754-8000.',
  [W('Heart Mountain Relocation Center'),L('Heart Mountain Foundation: Visit','https://www.heartmountain.org/visit/')],
  brick='some',hours='Mid-May to mid-October daily 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.; winter Wednesday to Saturday 10 to 5 (January to March Thursday to Saturday 10 to 4)'),
N('old-faithful-inn','Old Faithful Inn','Yellowstone National Park','wy','hotel','standing',44.45963,-110.83129,'exact','open','walk','hotels','1903–1904',
  'Robert Reamer, 29, designed the inn for the Yellowstone Park Company, and it was built over the winter of 1903-1904 of local lodgepole pine and rhyolite stone, opening in spring 1904 with electric light and steam heat. It was the first of the great park lodges of the West and is one of the few log hotels still standing in the country.',
  'The log-and-limb lobby rising through four stories of balconies (the lower two open to the public), the stone fireplace 16 feet square at the base, and the hand-made clock of copper, wood and wrought iron. The Old House is Reamer\'s original wing.',
  'The log lobby that set the style for park lodges.',
  'Yellowstone National Park, at the Old Faithful geyser; Yellowstone National Park Lodges runs it. Open April 30 to October 11, 2027, dates subject to change (operator\'s page, read November 2026). A park entrance pass is required. Lots along Old Faithful Inn Road and Geyser Avenue. No internet at the inn.',
  [W('Old Faithful Inn'),L('Yellowstone National Park Lodges: Old Faithful Inn','https://www.yellowstonenationalparklodges.com/lodgings/hotel/old-faithful-inn/'),L('NPS: Yellowstone fees','https://www.nps.gov/yell/planyourvisit/fees.htm')],
  th=[44.46065,-110.8317],thn='Lot on Old Faithful Inn Road',brick='no',hours='2027 season: April 30 to October 11 (subject to change)'),
N('trail-end-sheridan','Trail End (Kendrick Mansion)','Sheridan','wy','landmark','standing',44.80142,-106.9662,'exact','open','drive','','1908–1913',
  'The cattleman John B. Kendrick built this house between 1908 and 1913 to designs by Glenn Charles MacAlister, with interiors by D. Everett Waid of New York, for $164,000. Kendrick became governor in 1914 and a U.S. senator three years later, and the house became his summer home. The Sheridan County Historical Society bought it in 1969 to save it and opened it as a museum.',
  'A 13,748-square-foot Flemish Revival house; Wyoming State Parks says its rooms are furnished as in 1913-1933. Inside during open hours; the grounds open dawn to dusk.',
  'A cattle baron\'s mansion, furnished as he left it.',
  'Trail End State Historic Site, 400 Clarendon Avenue, Sheridan. Phone 307-674-4589. Hours from Wyoming State Parks (read November 2026); the house closes from December 15 to March 31 by those dates. Street parking (the nearest mapped lot is a school\'s). Fees not checked.',
  [W('Trail End State Historic Site'),L('Wyoming State Parks: About Trail End',WSP+'about-trail-end')],
  brick='?',hours='April-May and September to December 14: daily 1 to 4 p.m. June to August: daily 9 a.m. to 6 p.m. Grounds dawn to dusk'),
N('sheridan-inn','Sheridan Inn','Sheridan','wy','hotel','standing',44.80642,-106.95392,'exact','open','drive','hotels','1893–',
  'Thomas R. Kimball of Omaha designed the inn for the Chicago, Burlington and Quincy Railroad in 1893, after hotels he had seen in Scotland. Buffalo Bill Cody managed it for the railroad from 1894 to 1896, ran a stage line from it to Deadwood and auditioned acts for his Wild West Show from the porch. Condemned in 1967, it was saved by Neltje Doubleday Kings and later a nonprofit; it closed in 2012, was bought in 2013 and let rooms again from May 2015.',
  'A three-story wood-frame building 145 feet long under a gambrel roof, with porches 30 feet deep on two sides and a dormer for each of the 64 old bedrooms. The barns and livery stable behind it are gone.',
  'The railroad hotel Buffalo Bill ran.',
  'Working hotel at 856 Broadway Street, Sheridan, beside the old railroad yards. Phone 307-674-2178. Lot on North Main Street.',
  [W('Sheridan Inn'),L('Sheridan Inn','https://www.sheridaninn.com/')],
  th=[44.8063,-106.95543],thn='Lot on North Main Street',brick='?',hours='Daily 6 a.m. to 10 p.m. (its page)'),
N('fort-phil-kearny','Fort Phil Kearny','Banner','wy','fort','traces',44.53147,-106.82547,'site','open','walk','forts','1866–1868',
  'Colonel Henry B. Carrington\'s 18th Infantry began the fort on July 13, 1866, to guard the Bozeman Trail; building took over 600,000 board feet of lumber and 130,000 adobe bricks. The Fetterman and Wagon Box fights were fought within five miles. The state now keeps the fort and both battle sites.',
  'The fort\'s buildings are gone: a self-guided tour marks their archaeological remains, and a CCC-built cabin is furnished as officers\' and NCO quarters. Interpretive trails at the two battle sites. Visitor center with exhibits.',
  'The Bozeman Trail fort and the battlefields round it.',
  'Fort Phil Kearny State Historic Site, 528 Wagon Box Road, Banner. The fort grounds and the Fetterman and Wagon Box sites are open daily all year (Wyoming State Parks, read November 2026). Phone 307-684-7629. Lot on Wagon Box Road. Fees not checked.',
  [W('Fort Phil Kearny'),L('Wyoming State Parks: About Fort Phil Kearny',WSP+'about-fort-phil-kearny')],
  th=[44.53312,-106.82822],thn='Fort Phil Kearny lot, Wagon Box Road',brick='no',hours='Visitor center June to September daily 8 a.m. to 5 p.m.; October to May Wednesday to Sunday noon to 4. Grounds daily all year'),
N('wyoming-territorial-prison','Wyoming Territorial Prison','Laramie','wy','inst','standing',41.31148,-105.60985,'site','open','walk','','1872–',
  'The territorial penitentiary was built in 1872, on open land west of Laramie, and took its first prisoners early in 1873; it is one of the oldest buildings in Wyoming. Convicts built the warden\'s house in 1875. After the prison closed the University of Wyoming ran the site for most of its life as an experimental stock farm. It opened to the public in 1991 and became a state historic site in 2004.',
  'The main buildings are rough gray sandstone with brown sandstone corners and arches. Self-guided tours in open hours, guided tours usually late May to early September. Frontier Town, Ranchland and the Boxcar House close in winter.',
  'A frontier prison of gray sandstone.',
  'Wyoming Territorial Prison State Historic Site, 975 Snowy Range Road, Laramie. Admission (Wyoming State Parks, read November 2026) adults $9, ages 12-17 $4.50, 11 and under free; last ticket an hour before closing; closed Thanksgiving, Christmas and New Year\'s Day. Phone 307-745-6161. Parking at the site.',
  [W('Wyoming Territorial Prison State Historic Site'),L('Wyoming State Parks: About Wyoming Territorial Prison',WSP+'about-wyoming-territorial-prison')],
  brick='no',hours='May to September daily 9 a.m. to 4 p.m.; October to April Thursday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 3 p.m.'),
N('ames-monument','Ames Monument','Buford','wy','landmark','standing',41.13107,-105.39818,'exact','public','drive','','1882',
  'The Union Pacific finished this granite pyramid, 60 feet high and 60 feet square, in 1882 for $65,000, at 8,247 feet, the highest point of the first transcontinental railroad. H.H. Richardson designed it in memory of the financiers Oakes and Oliver Ames, and the Norcross Brothers built it from rough granite quarried half a mile away; Augustus Saint-Gaudens carved the portrait reliefs. In 1901 the line was moved several miles south and the pyramid was left alone on the summit.',
  'The pyramid of rough-hewn granite blocks, some of several tons, with the Ames brothers\' faces in relief and the lettering on the north side, which once faced the tracks. The little cemetery of the vanished town of Sherman is nearby (Wikipedia; not located here).',
  'A Richardson pyramid at the top of the transcontinental line.',
  'Ames Monument State Historic Site, south of I-80 at the Vedauwoo exit, about 20 miles east of Laramie. Open daily, sunrise to sunset (Wyoming State Parks, read November 2026). Small lot on Monument Road.',
  [W('Ames Monument'),L('Wyoming State Parks: Ames Monument',WSP+'places-to-go/ames-monument')],
  th=[41.13081,-105.39766],thn='Ames Monument lot, Monument Road',brick='no',hours='Daily, sunrise to sunset'),
N('fort-caspar','Fort Caspar','Casper','wy','fort','standing',42.83632,-106.37076,'site','open','walk','forts','1859–1867',
  'Louis Guinard built a toll bridge and trading post here in 1859 at the old Mormon Ferry crossing of the North Platte: Platte Bridge Station. Several thousand Cheyenne and Sioux attacked it in July 1865, and the post was renamed for Lieutenant Caspar Collins, killed in the fight. The Army left in August 1867. The fort was partly rebuilt in 1936 from sketches Collins made in 1863.',
  'Reconstructed log buildings and a wooden stockade (1936), and the city\'s regional history museum. The fort buildings are open May to September; in winter the grounds stay open but the buildings close.',
  'The bridge station on the North Platte that gave Casper its name.',
  'Fort Caspar Museum, 4001 Fort Caspar Road, Casper, run by the City of Casper. Entrance at Collins Street and Wyoming Boulevard. Admission May to September adults $5, with lower rates for others; half price October to April (museum page, read November 2026). Leashed pets on the fort grounds only. Phone (307) 235-8462.',
  [W('Fort Caspar'),L('Fort Caspar Museum','https://www.fortcasparwyoming.com/')],
  brick='no',hours='May to September daily 8 a.m. to 5 p.m. (fort buildings 8:30 to 4:30). October to April Tuesday to Saturday 8 to 5, fort buildings closed'),
N('fort-fetterman','Fort Fetterman','Douglas','wy','fort','ruins',42.84273,-105.48602,'site','open','walk','forts','1867–1882',
  'The 4th Infantry set up the post on July 19, 1867, as a supply base on the plains, and named it for Captain William J. Fetterman. Crook\'s and Mackenzie\'s expeditions of 1876 set out from here. The Army left in 1882, and the town of Fetterman City faded after Douglas was founded eleven miles away in 1886.',
  'An interpretive trail among the ruins of the fort\'s buildings; an officers\' quarters and the ordnance warehouse are restored and hold exhibits and dioramas.',
  'A plains supply fort, two buildings standing among the ruins.',
  'Fort Fetterman State Historic Site, 752 Wyoming 93, about 11 miles northwest of Douglas. Open only from Memorial Day weekend to Labor Day weekend; closed for the 2026 season (Wyoming State Parks, read November 2026). Phone 307-358-9288. Fees not checked; whether the grounds may be walked out of season was not checked.',
  [W('Fort Fetterman'),L('Wyoming State Parks: About Fort Fetterman',WSP+'about-fort-fetterman')],
  brick='?',hours='Memorial Day weekend to Labor Day weekend, Tuesday to Saturday 9 a.m. to 4 p.m.'),
N('cheyenne-depot','Union Pacific depot, Cheyenne (Cheyenne Depot Museum)','Cheyenne','wy','rail','standing',41.13182,-104.81431,'exact','open','walk','stations','1886–1887',
  'The Union Pacific began this depot in 1886, laying the cornerstone in mid-July, and finished it in 1887 to designs by Henry Van Brunt, at the foot of Capitol Avenue facing the State Capitol. It is built of stone brought by train from the railroad\'s own quarry, which was reopened for matching stone when an addition was built. It is a National Historic Landmark, and the museum calls it the last grand station left on the transcontinental route.',
  'The stone depot and its plaza; inside, the museum of Cheyenne and the Union Pacific and a narrow-gauge model railroad. The museum offers occasional tours of other railroad buildings (a steam shop tour was listed for December 12, 2026).',
  'The grand depot at the foot of Capitol Avenue.',
  'Cheyenne Depot Museum, 121 West 15th Street, a private nonprofit. Admission (its page, read November 2026) adults $10, 60 and over $9, ages 13-18 $5, 12 and under free; closed November 26 and 27, 2026. Downtown lots on West Lincolnway.',
  [W('Cheyenne Depot Museum'),L('Cheyenne Depot Museum','https://www.cheyennedepotmuseum.org/')],
  th=[41.13263,-104.8174],thn='City Center Parking, 322 West Lincolnway',brick='?',hours='Monday to Friday 9 a.m. to 5 p.m., Saturday 9 to 3, Sunday 11 to 3'),
]
